## Dashboard Component Architecture

Cloud Burst follows a structured approach to dashboard components to ensure consistency, maintainability, and a professional user experience. This document outlines the standards and patterns for building dashboard components for the event organizer experience.

### Core Dashboard Layout

The dashboard uses a responsive layout structure:

1. **Top Navigation**: Contains branding, search, and user profile
2. **Sidebar**: Contains navigation between dashboard sections
3. **Content Area**: Contains the main dashboard content
4. **Contextual Actions**: Contains actions relevant to the current view

#### Layout Example

```tsx
// src/app/protected/dashboard/page.tsx
import { DashboardHeader } from "@/components/dashboard/DashboardHeader";
import { DashboardStats } from "@/components/dashboard/DashboardStats";
import { RecentEvents } from "@/components/dashboard/RecentEvents";
import { ActivityFeed } from "@/components/dashboard/ActivityFeed";
import { QuickActions } from "@/components/dashboard/QuickActions";

export default function DashboardPage() {
  return (
    <div className="space-y-6 p-6">
      <DashboardHeader
        title="Dashboard"
        description="Welcome back, Organizer User"
        actions={<CreateEventButton />}
      />
      
      <DashboardStats />
      
      <div className="grid grid-cols-1 gap-6 md:grid-cols-2">
        <RecentEvents />
        <div className="space-y-6">
          <ActivityFeed />
          <QuickActions />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  );
}
```

### Card Components

Dashboard cards are used to contain discrete sections of content. They follow a consistent structure:

#### Card Structure

1. **Header**: Title, icon, and optional actions
2. **Content**: Main content of the card
3. **Footer**: Optional metadata or actions

#### Card Example

```tsx
// src/components/dashboard/StatCard.tsx
import { Card, CardContent, CardDescription, CardHeader, CardTitle } from "@/components/ui/card";

interface StatCardProps {
  title: string;
  value: string | number;
  description?: string;
  icon?: React.ReactNode;
  trend?: {
    value: number;
    isPositive: boolean;
  };
}

export function StatCard({ title, value, description, icon, trend }: StatCardProps) {
  return (
    <Card>
      <CardHeader className="flex flex-row items-center justify-between pb-2">
        <CardTitle className="text-sm font-medium">{title}</CardTitle>
        {icon && <div className="h-4 w-4 text-muted-foreground">{icon}</div>}
      </CardHeader>
      <CardContent>
        <div className="text-2xl font-bold">{value}</div>
        {description && (
          <CardDescription>{description}</CardDescription>
        )}
        {trend && (
          <p className={`text-xs ${trend.isPositive ? 'text-green-500' : 'text-red-500'}`}>
            {trend.isPositive ? '+' : '-'}{Math.abs(trend.value)}%
          </p>
        )}
      </CardContent>
    </Card>
  );
}
```

### Data Visualization

For visualizing data in the dashboard, we use consistent patterns:

1. **Charts**: Using Recharts for data visualization
2. **Metrics**: Using stat cards for key performance indicators
3. **Tables**: Using data tables for structured information
4. **Activity Feeds**: Using timeline components for chronological activities

#### Chart Example

```tsx
// src/components/dashboard/EventsChart.tsx
import { 
  AreaChart, 
  Area, 
  XAxis, 
  YAxis, 
  CartesianGrid, 
  Tooltip, 
  ResponsiveContainer 
} from 'recharts';
import { Card, CardContent, CardHeader, CardTitle } from '@/components/ui/card';

interface EventsChartProps {
  data: {
    date: string;
    value: number;
  }[];
  title: string;
}

export function EventsChart({ data, title }: EventsChartProps) {
  return (
    <Card>
      <CardHeader>
        <CardTitle>{title}</CardTitle>
      </CardHeader>
      <CardContent>
        <div className="h-[300px]">
          <ResponsiveContainer width="100%" height="100%">
            <AreaChart
              data={data}
              margin={{ top: 10, right: 30, left: 0, bottom: 0 }}
            >
              <defs>
                <linearGradient id="colorValue" x1="0" y1="0" x2="0" y2="1">
                  <stop offset="5%" stopColor="#8884d8" stopOpacity={0.8} />
                  <stop offset="95%" stopColor="#8884d8" stopOpacity={0} />
                </linearGradient>
              </defs>
              <CartesianGrid strokeDasharray="3 3" />
              <XAxis dataKey="date" />
              <YAxis />
              <Tooltip />
              <Area
                type="monotone"
                dataKey="value"
                stroke="#8884d8"
                fillOpacity={1}
                fill="url(#colorValue)"
              />
            </AreaChart>
          </ResponsiveContainer>
        </div>
      </CardContent>
    </Card>
  );
}
```

### Specialized Dashboard Components

#### Activity Feed

The Activity Feed component displays recent activities:

```tsx
// src/components/dashboard/ActivityFeed.tsx
import { Card, CardContent, CardHeader, CardTitle } from "@/components/ui/card";

interface Activity {
  id: string;
  type: 'photo' | 'comment' | 'rsvp' | 'system';
  content: string;
  timestamp: string;
  meta?: {
    eventId?: string;
    eventName?: string;
  };
}

interface ActivityFeedProps {
  activities: Activity[];
}

export function ActivityFeed({ activities }: ActivityFeedProps) {
  return (
    <Card>
      <CardHeader>
        <CardTitle>Activity Feed</CardTitle>
      </CardHeader>
      <CardContent className="max-h-[400px] overflow-auto">
        <div className="space-y-4">
          {activities.map((activity) => (
            <div key={activity.id} className="flex items-start gap-3">
              <ActivityIcon type={activity.type} />
              <div>
                <p className="text-sm">{activity.content}</p>
                {activity.meta?.eventName && (
                  <p className="text-xs text-muted-foreground">
                    in {activity.meta.eventName}
                  </p>
                )}
                <p className="text-xs text-muted-foreground">
                  {formatTimestamp(activity.timestamp)}
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </CardContent>
    </Card>
  );
}
```

#### Quick Actions

The Quick Actions component provides easy access to common tasks:

```tsx
// src/components/dashboard/QuickActions.tsx
import { Card, CardContent, CardHeader, CardTitle } from "@/components/ui/card";
import { Button } from "@/components/ui/button";
import Link from "next/link";

interface QuickAction {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  icon: React.ReactNode;
  href: string;
}

export function QuickActions() {
  const actions: QuickAction[] = [
    {
      title: "Create Event",
      description: "Set up a new event",
      icon: <CalendarIcon />,
      href: "/protected/events/new",
    },
    {
      title: "Invite Attendees",
      description: "Send invitations to guests",
      icon: <UserPlusIcon />,
      href: "/protected/attendees/manage",
    },
    // Additional actions...
  ];

  return (
    <Card>
      <CardHeader>
        <CardTitle>Quick Actions</CardTitle>
      </CardHeader>
      <CardContent>
        <div className="grid gap-4">
          {actions.map((action) => (
            <Button
              key={action.title}
              variant="outline"
              className="justify-start h-auto p-4"
              asChild
            >
              <Link href={action.href}>
                <div className="flex items-center gap-4">
                  <div className="rounded-full p-2 bg-muted">
                    {action.icon}
                  </div>
                  <div>
                    <div className="font-medium">{action.title}</div>
                    <div className="text-xs text-muted-foreground">
                      {action.description}
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </Link>
            </Button>
          ))}
        </div>
      </CardContent>
    </Card>
  );
}
```

### Data States

All dashboard components should handle various data states:

#### Empty State

```tsx
// Example of an empty state component
export function EmptyState({ 
  title = "No items found",
  description = "Get started by creating your first item.",
  action,
  icon
}: EmptyStateProps) {
  return (
    <div className="flex flex-col items-center justify-center p-8 text-center">
      {icon && <div className="mb-4 text-muted-foreground">{icon}</div>}
      <h3 className="text-lg font-medium">{title}</h3>
      <p className="mt-1 text-sm text-muted-foreground">{description}</p>
      {action && <div className="mt-6">{action}</div>}
    </div>
  );
}
```

#### Loading State

```tsx
// Example of a loading state component
export function LoadingState() {
  return (
    <div className="space-y-4">
      <div className="h-8 w-1/3 rounded-md bg-muted animate-pulse" />
      <div className="h-20 rounded-md bg-muted animate-pulse" />
      <div className="h-20 rounded-md bg-muted animate-pulse" />
      <div className="h-20 rounded-md bg-muted animate-pulse" />
    </div>
  );
}
```

#### Error State

```tsx
// Example of an error state component
export function ErrorState({ 
  title = "Something went wrong",
  description = "There was an error loading this content.",
  retryAction
}: ErrorStateProps) {
  return (
    <div className="flex flex-col items-center justify-center p-8 text-center">
      <AlertTriangleIcon className="h-8 w-8 text-red-500 mb-4" />
      <h3 className="text-lg font-medium">{title}</h3>
      <p className="mt-1 text-sm text-muted-foreground">{description}</p>
      {retryAction && <div className="mt-6">{retryAction}</div>}
    </div>
  );
}
```

## Implementation Checklist

When creating dashboard components:

- [ ] Use the appropriate card structure
- [ ] Implement all data states (loading, empty, error)
- [ ] Ensure responsive behavior across devices
- [ ] Implement proper accessibility attributes
- [ ] Use consistent spacing and typography
- [ ] Include appropriate actions and interactions
- [ ] Test in both light and dark mode

## Best Practices

1. **Consistent Layout**: Maintain consistent spacing and layout across all dashboard components
2. **Progressive Loading**: Implement skeleton loaders for a better user experience during data fetching
3. **Actionable Insights**: Make data actionable by including relevant actions with each component
4. **Responsive Design**: Ensure all components adapt appropriately to different screen sizes
5. **Accessibility**: Implement proper ARIA attributes and ensure keyboard navigability
6. **Performance**: Optimize rendering and data fetching to ensure a responsive dashboard
7. **Dark Mode Support**: Ensure all components look good in both light and dark mode 